#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334102 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfc0d9e1-bce1-5d62-b3c8-f7d7bb226497" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius and Interval of Convergence of a Power Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d52ece6-3507-5d0e-96ef-a407fd4aece3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R=5$ and $I=[-1,1]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334104} {:answer/id #uuid "d20827e2-817d-5248-8cf6-f330492e35d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R=1$ and $I=(-6,-4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334105} {:answer/id #uuid "ae1a09be-2fde-594c-b265-b7871cab732e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R=1$ and $I=[-6,-4]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334106} {:answer/id #uuid "50627ed6-6733-519f-9aa4-22157ae63515" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R=5$ and $I=(-1,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334107} {:answer/id #uuid "caeb5813-0b9c-50ed-b963-df1f7f195d41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R=1$ and $I=[-6,-4)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334108}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae1a09be-2fde-594c-b265-b7871cab732e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$R=1$ and $I=[-6,-4]$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334106} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e7d4214-9dca-5555-97a6-d8a944ed07b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334103}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1c3c2591-89b8-5e94-a7b0-e02f5b424693" :question/math-academy-id "q-103125" :question/problem "Find the radius of convergence $R$ and interval of convergence $I$ for the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(x+5)^{n}}{n(n+1)}\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(x+5)^{n}}{n(n+1)}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(x+5)^{n+1}}{(n+1)(n+2)}\\cdot \\frac{n(n+1)}{(x+5)^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{n(x+5)}{n+2}| \\\\  & =|x+5|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{n}{n+2} \\\\  & =|x+5|{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{(1+\\frac{2}{n})}) \\\\  & =|x+5|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =|x+5|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|x+5| & <1 \\\\ -1<x+5 & <1 \\\\ -6<x & <-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the radius of convergence is $R=1,$ and the series converges for any $x$ that lies in the open interval $(-6,-4).$\n\nThe ratio test gives no conclusion about the convergence of the series when $L=1.$ So, when $|x+5|=1,$ we do not know if the series converges. This means that the endpoints $x=-6$ and $x=-4$ need to be investigated separately using another test for convergence.\n\n- When $x=-6$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(x+5)^{n}}{n(n+1)} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-6+5)^{n}}{n(n+1)} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}+n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the alternating series test.\n\n- When $x=-4$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(x+5)^{n}}{n(n+1)} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-4+5)^{n}}{n(n+1)} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{1}^{n}}{{n}^{2}+n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}+n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the above series with  ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}},$\nwe see that the series is convergent by the comparison test.\n\nSo the interval of convergence is $I=[-6,-4].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334109 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60f61c26-ff2d-5343-becd-d8f44f623cd3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Convergence of a Power Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba31d5d8-018a-5a34-b32e-dd21454be4cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334111} {:answer/id #uuid "2843dd8f-9e62-5e53-8902-beabd38952a0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334112} {:answer/id #uuid "53895d66-6920-5109-ade7-555cb41a5340" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334113} {:answer/id #uuid "12fc2b45-e990-58cc-a67f-d574393e1770" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334114} {:answer/id #uuid "787b738a-3ae6-58c3-a271-a9d3586790f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334115}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "787b738a-3ae6-58c3-a271-a9d3586790f4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334115} :answer-field/id #uuid "2801205e-50a3-50aa-9a96-88f02219f4e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334110}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "324176f6-84c3-5e78-83c2-c6f01c61d811" :question/math-academy-id "q-103170" :question/problem "Given that the power series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}(x+7)^{n}$ converges at  $x=-3,$ which of the following **must** be true?\n\n- The series converges at $x=-11$\n- The series converges at $x=-8$\n- The series converges at $x=1$" :question/worked-solution "The center of the expansion is $x=-7,$ and since the series is convergent for $x=-3,$ this means that the radius of convergence $R$ is *at least* $4,$ i.e., $R\\ge 4.$ So, the series is convergent for every point in the interval $(-11,-3].$ However, we know nothing about the convergence of the series at any point outside the interval $(-11,-3].$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057019/assets/4764256e5f00e7a11d688bd99d5fa1c22db819e7a188113f0ef4cd987a9b7595.png)\n\n \n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $-11\\notin (-11,-3],$ we cannot guarantee that the series is convergent at $x=-11.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Since $-8\\in (-11,-3],$ the series is convergent at $x=-8.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $1\\notin (-11,-3],$ we cannot guarantee that the series is convergent at $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334116 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfc0d9e1-bce1-5d62-b3c8-f7d7bb226497" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius and Interval of Convergence of a Power Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2057098c-1421-5822-8c4c-e50a006401e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334118} {:answer/id #uuid "002e940d-108e-52e1-b4ee-6923056c0ef3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334119} {:answer/id #uuid "73b6a948-b351-5c93-80ab-951908e2a5ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334120} {:answer/id #uuid "c90bdf9a-83ed-53e6-83d7-d2162fbfbea2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334121} {:answer/id #uuid "65aa1b93-5a65-5c32-914d-1893a6a36ae4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334122}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c90bdf9a-83ed-53e6-83d7-d2162fbfbea2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334121} :answer-field/id #uuid "24582413-8748-5137-837c-d445abccfe96" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334117}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1aa775ab-4fbe-5591-b31a-ed5f34a4899c" :question/math-academy-id "q-10947" :question/problem "Find the radius of convergence $R$ for the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{2}^{n}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{n}=1+2(x-\\frac{1}{2})+{2}^{2}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{2}^{3}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}+⋯\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test we define ${a}_{n}={2}^{n}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{n}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{2}^{n+1}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{n+1}}{{2}^{n}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }2|x-\\frac{1}{2}| \\\\  & =2|x-\\frac{1}{2}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied if $|x-\\frac{1}{2}|<\\frac{1}{2}.$ Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334123 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfc0d9e1-bce1-5d62-b3c8-f7d7bb226497" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Radius and Interval of Convergence of a Power Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6e0c872-b869-5b04-9e2a-ed68f8050ddc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334125} {:answer/id #uuid "39421ad5-87cf-5146-b507-c895a3b82c2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334126} {:answer/id #uuid "c1db1ff7-3ec0-5d9a-9442-fe14b6fff0b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334127} {:answer/id #uuid "d89b326c-c4a4-535e-937d-419549c4d26f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334128} {:answer/id #uuid "eaa74f21-2175-5b38-8fa9-ba6d379ea3ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334129}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c1db1ff7-3ec0-5d9a-9442-fe14b6fff0b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "R=\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334127} :answer-field/id #uuid "243d8ed7-e88f-5334-91a0-c36637d6a7b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334124}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "356c5bd2-86e1-54eb-8ef4-0efdc705efc6" :question/math-academy-id "q-13835" :question/problem "Find the radius of convergence $R$ for the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(x-1)^{3n}}{{n}^{n}}=(x-1)^{3}+\\frac{(x-1)^{6}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{(x-1)^{9}}{{3}^{3}}+⋯\\,.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(x-1)^{3n}}{{n}^{n}}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(x-1)^{3(n+1)}}{(n+1)^{n+1}}\\cdot \\frac{{n}^{n}}{(x-1)^{3n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(x-1)^{3}}{(n+1)}\\cdot {(\\frac{n}{n+1})}^{n}| \\\\  & =|x-1{|}^{3}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{(n+1)}\\cdot {(\\frac{n}{n+1})}^{n} \\\\  & =|x-1{|}^{3}\\cdot 0\\cdot {e}^{-1} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for all $-\\infty <x<\\infty .$ Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\infty .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186334130 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60f61c26-ff2d-5343-becd-d8f44f623cd3" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Convergence of a Power Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f78d9a50-d805-55c9-b9ff-2f8041fa5d75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334132} {:answer/id #uuid "a982613e-36c7-5790-b6aa-33a7bc13604e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334133} {:answer/id #uuid "e6d783be-753c-53f0-a731-6b845194dd0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334134} {:answer/id #uuid "ad60f04c-0dec-5a9c-9dcf-f5e33d616a00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334135} {:answer/id #uuid "b847c8a6-18b8-5222-99dc-e1d375b5d180" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334136}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f78d9a50-d805-55c9-b9ff-2f8041fa5d75" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334132} :answer-field/id #uuid "71e934e2-1c39-54b5-b358-71cc23aaf77e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334131}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4b3ec342-e185-541d-8e76-70ddd79f6b72" :question/math-academy-id "q-50877" :question/problem "The power series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}(x-3)^{n}$ converges at  $x=-2.$ Which of the following **must** be true?\n\n- The series converges at $x=-3$\n- The series diverges at $x=1$\n- The series converges at $x=8$" :question/worked-solution "The center of the expansion is $x=3,$ and since the series is convergent for $x=-2,$ this means that the radius of convergence $R$ is *at least* $5,$ i.e., $R\\ge 5.$ So, the series is convergent for every point in the interval $[-2,8).$ However, we know nothing about the convergence of the series at any point outside the interval $[-2,8).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14057019/assets/38763aa0d87065a01ccdf0d94bca2a4e1d6ef227aeab4aaacb9703246eee6abf.png)\n\n \n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $-3\\notin [-2,8),$ we cannot guarantee that the series is convergent at $x=-3.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $1\\in [-2,8),$ the series is convergent at $x=1.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $8\\notin [-2,8),$  we cannot guarantee that the series is convergent at $x=8.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the given statements must be true.\""}]}
